---
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { repoUrl, SITE } from '@/config';
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '@/lib/seo';
import { url } from '@/lib/urls';

const title = 'About this project';
const description =
  'Why this site exists, how the fixes are written and checked, who maintains it, and how to contribute. Open source under the MIT license.';
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'About', path: '/about/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} og="about" schema={[breadcrumbs(crumbs, Astro.site)]}>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        React is easy to start and surprisingly easy to slow down or subtly break. This project collects the issues
        experienced React engineers keep fixing, and teaches the reasoning behind each fix, both to people and to Claude.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose about">
      <h2>Two ways to use the same knowledge</h2>
      <p>
        The project started as <a href={repoUrl('skills', 'tree')} rel="noopener">fifteen Agent Skills</a>: folders of
        instructions, worked examples and tested code that Claude loads when a task calls for them. This site turns every
        rule in those skills into a page a person can read: what you see, why React behaves that way, the fix, and how to
        confirm it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you use Claude Code, <a href={url('/how-it-works/')}>install the skills</a> and Claude applies the fixes in your
        code. If you don’t, the site stands on its own.
      </p>

      <h2>How the fixes are written</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Symptom first.</strong> Each page is titled with what you’d see or search for, not with the name of the
          solution.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Explained with a small mental model.</strong> Re-renders start from state changes; identity is type,
          position and key; every closure sees one render. The same dozen facts explain almost every fix.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Structural fixes before patches.</strong> Moving state, passing children and fixing keys come before
          <code>memo</code>, <code>z-index: 9999</code> or a <code>setTimeout</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verifiable.</strong> Every fix says how to confirm it: a Profiler recording, a Performance trace or a
          test.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Traceable.</strong> Every page links to the skill file it’s drawn from, and the demos import the same
          tested code the skills ship.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Versions</h2>
      <p>
        The advice targets React 18 and 19, including React 19.2 and the React Compiler, and works with Next.js, Vite,
        Remix, React Router and React Native (except the DOM-specific parts). Pages name the version when an API needs a
        specific one.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics, no cookies, no tracking. Your browser stores two things locally: your light or dark theme choice,
        and your ticks on the <a href={url('/checklist/')}>review checklist</a>. Neither leaves your device.
      </p>

      <h2>Who maintains it</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={SITE.author.url} rel="noopener">{SITE.author.name}</a> builds and maintains the skills and this site. Found
        a mistake, or a React issue that deserves a page? <a href={`${SITE.repo.url}/issues`} rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>
        or send a pull request: each fix is one MDX file in <code>site/src/content/fixes</code>, and the
        <a href={repoUrl('site/README.md')} rel="noopener">authoring guide</a> explains the format.
      </p>

      <h2>License</h2>
      <p>
        The skills, the code and this site’s content are released under the <a href={repoUrl('LICENSE')} rel="noopener"
          >MIT license</a
        >. You can use, copy, modify and redistribute them, including commercially, as long as the copyright notice and
        license text are kept.
      </p>
      <p class="disclaimer">
        React is a trademark of Meta Platforms, Inc. This project is independent and isn’t affiliated with or endorsed by
        Meta or the React team. Claude is a product of Anthropic.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .about h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0.5em;
  }

  .disclaimer {
    color: var(--text-3);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
</style>
